arm64: dts: h713: add the AV1 decoder node

The H713 carries an AV1 decoder nobody has ever read a register from.  It is
Google IP rather than Allwinner's own or the VeriSilicon core mainline knows:
the stock DTB names it "allwinner,sunxi-google-ve" at 0x01c0d000, and the
vendor library libawav1.so is built around taffel::ctypes::SwRegisters and
BigSeaRegisters, which are Google AV1 codenames.  Mainline's only AV1 backend
is Rockchip's VPU981, whose register file reaches index 394 where this block's
holds 292 -- not the same core.  Full evidence in
docs/reference/vp9-av1-codec-scouting-2026-09-24.md.

That the SoC design includes the block is already established from our own
side: the CCU carries bus_av1, mbus_av1 and RST_BUS_AV1, and the PPU driver
enumerates an AV1 power domain that the board reports, off only because nothing
claims it.  None of that proves this package has the silicon, because no
register has ever been read.  This node exists so a probe driver can do that.

The node is DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN STOCK.  The vendor also lists "bus_ve"
and "reset_ve", and both belong to cedrus.  A clock is refcounted and can be
shared; a reset cannot, and deasserting reset_ve from here could reset the
video engine underneath a running decode.  bus_av1 and mbus_av1 are this
block's own and reset_av1 has no other consumer, so only those are claimed.  If
the ID reads as garbage, the next thing to try is adding bus_ve as a shared
clock -- not reset_ve.

The "av1" module clock the stock node lists is absent from our CCU and is not
required here: it drives the decode core, while register access needs only the
bus clock.  Adding it means identifying its gate register, which cannot be done
read-only because a gated sunxi CCU register reads zero.

The compatible is ours rather than the vendor's, so this cannot collide with a
future real driver.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -1350,6 +1350,48 @@
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * The AV1 decoder -- Google IP, not Allwinner's and not
+		 * VeriSilicon's. The stock DTB calls it
+		 * compatible = "allwinner,sunxi-google-ve" at this address, and
+		 * libawav1.so is built around taffel::ctypes::SwRegisters and
+		 * BigSeaRegisters, which are Google AV1 codenames. There is no
+		 * driver for it in any tree; mainline's only AV1 backend is
+		 * Rockchip's VPU981, whose register file reaches 394 where this
+		 * block's holds 292. See
+		 * docs/reference/vp9-av1-codec-scouting-2026-09-24.md.
+		 *
+		 * This node exists so a probe driver can read the core ID and
+		 * settle two questions at once: whether the block is alive on
+		 * this die, and which of its two register-file variants this is
+		 * (AsicRegisterMapWidth picks 1168 or 816 bytes by comparing an
+		 * ID against 0xb16c).
+		 *
+		 * DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN STOCK. The vendor node also lists
+		 * "bus_ve" and "reset_ve", and both are cedrus's. A reset is not
+		 * safely shared: deasserting reset_ve here could reset the video
+		 * engine underneath a running decode. bus_av1 and mbus_av1 are
+		 * this block's own, and reset_av1 has no other consumer, so the
+		 * probe takes only those. If the ID reads as garbage, adding
+		 * bus_ve as a SHARED CLOCK (refcounted, unlike a reset) is the
+		 * next thing to try -- not reset_ve.
+		 *
+		 * The "av1" module clock the stock node lists is absent from our
+		 * CCU and is deliberately not required here: it drives the
+		 * decode core, while register access needs only the bus clock.
+		 */
+		av1: av1@1c0d000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h713-av1-probe";
+			reg = <0x01c0d000 0x1000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 107 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_AV1>, <&ccu CLK_MBUS_AV1>;
+			clock-names = "bus", "mbus";
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_AV1>;
+			iommus = <&iommu 5 1>;
+			power-domains = <&ppu 4>;
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+
 		/* === GPU (Mali-G31 Bifrost, Panfrost driver) === */
 		gpu: gpu@1800000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h616-mali",
